  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1235 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nA surface cold front is forecast to move through South Texas today \nthough this will simply be a slight wind shift and not so much a \ndrop in temperatures. Ahead of this frontal passage, moisture \nconvergence, combined with an upper-level disturbance moving across \nSouth Texas are leading to showers and storms as of this writing and \ncontinuing through sunrise. By this point, the wind shift associated \nwith the front will start ushering in drier air with a thin line of \nweak showers possible along this wind shift. By in all, today is \nlooking to be a seasonable day with mostly cloudy conditions in the \nmorning and becoming more scattered/partly cloudy this \nafternoon and into tonight.\n\nMeanwhile, a mid to upper-level low is digging into the northwestern \nMexican states along with the subtropical jet streak positioning the \nright entrance region over much of South Texas Saturday afternoon \nand into Sunday. What is a tad bit different in this forecast \ncompared to 24 hrs ago is that PWATs only rebound to 1.5-1.7\" \n(according to the GEFS), and up to 1.9\" per the ECMWF Ensemble. This, \ncombined with differences in overall strength of the upper level low \nis leading to the spread in rainfall totals from Saturday through \nMonday, but the overall consensus is that 1-3 inches of rainfall \namounts are forecast with the highest rainfall totals across the \nBrush Country and toward the Baffin Bay area. Locations east of the \nI-37 corridor will be on the lower end of the forecast rainfall \namounts (as high as 1.5 inches, but generally under an inch for most \nlocations). The general limiting factor for higher rainfall totals \nwill be due to overall northeasterly flow (aiding in isentropic \nascent, but more of a stabilizing force). With lower instability \nfrom this flow, the less likely that storms will be able to generate \nhigher rainfall rates. Regardless, the WPC has placed all of South \nTexas under a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall (level 1 of 4). \nIf there is an upside to this rainfall is that it will be spread out \nover roughly a 48 hr period, allowing our anomalously dry soils to \nsoak in the rainfall. Chances for rain continue into Monday, but \nwill be ending from west to east Monday night as the upper level low \nstarts filling in and becomes more of a shortwave trough as it exits \nthe Lone Star State, with a brief period of quieter skies, but the \nsubtropical jet streak redevelops across the state from Wednesday, \nresulting in the return of 10-30% PoPs with a cold front once again \nforecast to move through on Thursday. While no significant drop in \ntemperatures are in store, it will be enough to keep the dewpoint \ntemperatures in the 50s and 60s. \n\nAlong the coastlines, confidence is growing in coastal flooding \nconcerns during high tide cycles. Saturday's high tide cycles are \nforecasting water levels reaching near 2.0 ft above MSL , but Sunday \nand Monday's high tides are forecast to push closer to 2.5 ft above \nMSL thanks to the near-gale conditions pushing water toward the \ncoastline and the arrival of the new moon phase, but forecast wave \nperiods of 6-7 seconds will be the limiting factor to the runup on \nGulf beaches. We're closely monitoring this risk along with the risk \nof near-gale conditions. We'll have the day shift look at this risk \ncloser for any headline issuance.",
